Most will get such a gpu to have decent fps in their favorite games

 @tracesmith3572  This is why chiplets or some kind of 3 dimensional circuitry needs to be figured out soon. I'm surprised no one has developed a double sided CPU/GPU where the chip's mounted to both sides of the substrate with I/O and power delivery in the center. This would allow cooling to access both sides.

We're literally reaching the physical limitations of copper on silicon without signal degradation or channel crosstalk because density is getting too tight to where electrons won't behave. And the ridiculous size of monolithic dies are pushing reticle limits.

I really think we might need to take a couple steps back before we'll be able to make another large leap forwards.

 @Unordinary-lg4yt  The stagnation will get even worse. Weren't you reading? Where reaching the limitations of physics when it comes to shrinking transistors.

Heavy parallel processing that you can spread across a huge substrate is helping to keep enterprise/datacenter moving forward but when it comes to latency intensive operations like games adding 50 cores does nothing. More vcache helped a lot with gaming and it's specific needs but it's already giving diminishing returns and does absolute dick for production. The 9800X3D's uplift can be attributed mostly to clockspeed increases because they swapped the stack of the vcache and cores, but they're not going to magically hit 7ghz with Zen6.

Some drastic new architecture or breakthrough is needed before we'll ever see the uplift we used to in the 2000s.

 @christroy8047  as much of a good value proposition this new card is, I believed an average user won't pair a $220 ( let's give the msrp benefit of doubt) gpu with an equally priced CPU like ryzen 5 5600, most likely a cpu around ryzen 5 3600.
